{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nBoth engines must produce the same verdict contract, so downstream readers (claim gate, `review-plan --status`, `sign-review`, MCP status tools, the sidecar readers) need no change and the two engines are directly comparable. The pipeline's verdict is built by `orchestrator.finalize_verdict` (`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/orchestrator.py:333`) and recorded by `sidecar.build_payload`/`sidecar.emit` (`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/sidecar.py:586`, `:58`) against `src/rebar/schemas/plan_review_verdict.schema.json` and `review_result.schema.json`. The agentic engine returns a different finding shape.\n\n## Approach\n\nAdd an adapter in the agentic module's package (`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/agentic_verdict.py`) that maps agentic findings onto the pipeline's finding and verdict shape, per ADR 0123:\n\n- Each finding's `decision`, the key `finalize_verdict`'s partition reads (`orchestrator.py:296-299`), is set from severity alone: `blocker` becomes `\"block\"`; `major`, `minor` and `trivial` become `\"advisory\"` (operator decision on the epic, 2026-11-09). Criterion posture does not change it. Zero blockers is PASS.\n- Field mapping: `title` + `description` become the finding text, `evidence` the evidence list, `suggested_fix` the remediation, `criteria` the criteria list, filtered to ids in `registry.by_id()` (an empty list is valid), `severity` is kept as an attribute; finding ids are minted with `orchestrator.mint_finding_id` (`orchestrator.py:105`) so dedup and prior-concern matching work unchanged.\n- A runner error or malformed output becomes INDETERMINATE with the same `resolution_class` the pipeline uses for a failed LLM pass, so `attest.sign_plan_review` (`attest.py:113`) refuses to sign it exactly as it refuses a degraded pipeline verdict.\n- Coverage records `engine: \"agentic\"` and the model that ran.\n\nThe adapted verdict then flows through the unchanged post-dispatch path in `_run_plan_review` (`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/__init__.py`, after `produce_plan_review_verdict`): Pass-3 floors, advisory cap, sidecar emit and signing. Mapping at the engine boundary, rather than teaching each reader a second shape, is what keeps every downstream reader untouched.\n\n## Scope\n\n- src/rebar/llm/plan_review/agentic_verdict.py (new)\n- src/rebar/llm/plan_review/agentic.py (call the adapter)\n- tests/unit/test_plan_review_agentic_verdict.py (new)\n- tests/unit/test_plan_review_verdict_schema_parity.py (new: both engines against the same schemas)\n\n## Testing\n\n- Worked example: findings `[{severity: \"blocker\", ...}, {severity: \"major\", ...}]` produce `verdict: BLOCK` with one `blocking` and one `advisory` entry; `[{severity: \"major\"}, {severity: \"trivial\"}]` produce PASS with two advisory entries; `[]` produces PASS with none.\n- Failure/edge: runner error and schema-invalid output produce INDETERMINATE that `sign_plan_review` refuses; an unknown severity value is treated as advisory and logged, never as blocking by default.\n- Schema parity: a pipeline verdict from `FakeRunner` and an agentic verdict both validate against `plan_review_verdict.schema.json`, and both sidecar payloads validate against `review_result.schema.json`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The severity-to-verdict and field mappings above are pinned by `tests/unit/test_plan_review_agentic_verdict.py`.\n- [ ] Both engines' verdicts and sidecars validate against the same schemas, pinned by `tests/unit/test_plan_review_verdict_schema_parity.py`.\n- [ ] `make verify` exits 0."}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1794257454419832001,"uuid":"c64a6c84-2f31-49ab-a0f2-e33321b5db5a"}